<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
=============================================================================
 MODULE DOCSTRING : services/account-service/pom.xml
=============================================================================

 Purpose
   Maven module descriptor for the CardDemo Account Service, the bounded
   context that owns the account, customer and card cross reference data of
   the AWS CardDemo mainframe to AWS migration. This file is the module's
   dependency contract and nothing else: every class under src/main/java and
   src/test/java compiles against exactly the artifact set declared below.
   Declaring too little breaks those compiles; declaring more violates an
   explicit architectural prohibition recorded at the point of exclusion.

 Bounded context and owned data
   PostgreSQL schema : account
   Tables            : accounts, customers, card_xref
   Record contracts  : app/cpy/CVACT01Y.cpy  ACCOUNT-RECORD    RECLN 300
                       app/cpy/CVCUS01Y.cpy  CUSTOMER-RECORD   RECLN 500
                       app/cpy/CVACT03Y.cpy  CARD-XREF-RECORD  RECLN 50
   Money contract    : ACCT-CURR-BAL PIC S9(10)V99 at app/cpy/CVACT01Y.cpy
                       line 7 is the normative exemplar. It becomes
                       NUMERIC(12,2) in SQL, BigDecimal at scale 2 with
                       RoundingMode.HALF_UP in Java, and a JSON string on the
                       wire so that no client can route it through an
                       IEEE 754 double.
   Secondary access  : a non unique index on card_xref.account_id replaces
                       the CXACAIX VSAM alternate index, described at
                       app/csd/CARDDEMO.CSD line 64 as the alternate index to
                       CCXREF via the account key.

 COBOL programs migrated into this module (REFERENCE ONLY, never modified)
   app/cbl/COACTVWC.cbl              941 lines, account view,   CICS CAVW
   app/cbl/COACTUPC.cbl             4236 lines, account update, CICS CAUP
   app/cbl/CBACT01C.cbl              430 lines, account file reader
   app/cbl/CBACT03C.cbl              178 lines, cross reference reader
   app/cbl/CBCUS01C.cbl              178 lines, customer reader
   app/app-vsam-mq/cbl/COACCT01.cbl  620 lines, MQ account inquiry

 Dependency contract
   Sibling modules   : common-lib, and ONLY common-lib. It is the one
                       permitted intra reactor dependency of this module, and
                       it supplies every shared contract listed at its
                       declaration below. No other service module may be
                       depended on from here, because importing another
                       bounded context's domain package would put two
                       deployables in charge of one table.
   Everything else   : third party artifacts whose versions are managed for
                       this module by the parent, enumerated once at their
                       declarations and never pinned again here.

 Parameters (all inherited, none restated in this file)
   groupId, version, java.version 21, maven.compiler.release 21, the source
   and reporting encodings, the three imported bills of materials, and every
   dependency and plugin version arrive from the parent aggregator at
   services/pom.xml. This module contributes none of them, which is why no
   groupId element, no version element on the project itself and no
   properties section appear below.

 Returns
   A Spring Boot executable jar under target/, named from this module's
   artifactId and its inherited version, produced by the repackage goal of
   spring-boot-maven-plugin. That jar is the single artifact the runtime
   stage of this module's Dockerfile copies.

 Errors
   The build fails, deliberately and with no tolerance, when the inherited
   maven-checkstyle-plugin execution named checkstyle-documentation-gate
   finds a missing or incomplete Javadoc comment, when Surefire reports a
   failing *Test, or when Failsafe reports a failing *RepositoryIT. No
   suppression, no relaxation and no severity downgrade of that gate appears
   anywhere in this file, and none may be added.

 Documentation convention
   Rule 1 "Explainability" governs this file. XML has no docstring construct,
   so the mandated analogue applies: this header block is the module
   docstring, and every non obvious declaration below carries an adjacent
   "WHY :" comment naming one of the four rule categories. The polyglot
   convention is written up in docs/CODE_DOCUMENTATION_STANDARD.md.

   Label form: this file uses the PLURAL labels "Alternatives Considered:",
   "Refactoring Rationale:", "Assumptions:" and "Trade-offs:", spelled as
   Rule 1 itself and the house convention in tests/README.md section 12
   spell them. config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml and its suppressions file use
   the singular in file forms instead; that variant is deliberately NOT
   followed here, so please do not harmonise this file toward it.

   Comment safety: XML 1.0 section 2.5 forbids a doubled hyphen anywhere
   inside a comment body, and a POM that breaks that rule fails to parse
   before any goal runs. Every ruler in this file is therefore drawn with
   "=" or "~", never with a hyphen, and every line range is written in the
   form "L669 to L756".
=============================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
    WHY : Assumptions: the explicit ../pom.xml relative path records that this
          module is only ever built inside the nine module reactor rooted at
          services/pom.xml. It is never resolved from a remote repository,
          because com.carddemo:carddemo-services is never published. Leaving
          the element off would make Maven probe the local and remote
          repositories for a parent that does not exist there, turning a
          missing sibling checkout into an opaque resolution failure instead
          of a plain "file not found".
    -->
    <parent>
        <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
        <artifactId>carddemo-services</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
        <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <!--
    WHY : Assumptions: groupId and version are omitted ON PURPOSE because both
          are inherited from the parent above. Restating either here would
          create a second place that has to be edited at every version bump,
          and the two would drift silently the first time only one was
          updated. The artifactId matches the parent's modules entry character
          for character, which is what lets "-pl account-service" select this
          module in the reactor and in the CI workflow.
    -->
    <artifactId>account-service</artifactId>

    <!--
    WHY : Assumptions: the deployment unit is a container image whose runtime
          stage copies one self contained jar and starts it with "java -jar".
          A war would require a servlet container in the image, and the plain
          library packaging the repackage goal starts from would produce a jar
          with no embedded server and no manifest main class, so the container
          would build successfully and then fail to start.
    -->
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>CardDemo Account Service</name>
    <description>
        Account bounded context of the AWS CardDemo mainframe to AWS
        migration. Owns the account, customer and card cross reference data in
        PostgreSQL schema "account" (tables accounts, customers, card_xref)
        and migrates six COBOL programs: COACTVWC (account view), COACTUPC
        (account update), CBACT01C (account file reader), CBACT03C (card cross
        reference reader), CBCUS01C (customer reader) and COACCT01 (the MQ
        account inquiry extension).
    </description>

    <dependencies>

        <!--
        =============================================================================
         Shared kernel: the ONE permitted intra reactor dependency
        =============================================================================
        WHY : Assumptions: common-lib is the only sibling module this one is
              allowed to depend on, and it supplies every shared contract this
              module consumes: money/Money and money/MoneyModule,
              codec/FixedWidthCodec, codec/ZonedDecimalCodec,
              codec/CopybookLayout, error/ApiError,
              error/GlobalExceptionHandler, error/AbendDetail,
              web/PageResponse, web/CorrelationIdFilter,
              security/JwtRoleConverter, observability/MetricsConfig,
              time/TimestampFormatter, validation/DateEditValidator and
              validation/FieldValidationFlag. Each former COBOL COPY of a
              shared concern resolves to exactly one type in that package and
              is never re declared per service, which is the Java analogue of
              compiling every program against a single copybook include path.
        WHY : Assumptions: common-lib's GlobalExceptionHandler ALREADY maps
              OptimisticLockException to HTTP 409 Conflict, which is precisely
              the contract the account update path needs, so this module must
              not re implement it. COACTUPC carries its own before image
              comparison across the pseudo conversational gap: ACUP-OLD-DETAILS
              at app/cbl/COACTUPC.cbl line 669, the 1205-COMPARE-OLD-NEW
              paragraph, WS-DATACHANGED-FLAG at line 168 with its 88 level
              DATA-WAS-CHANGED-BEFORE-UPDATE at line 521, and SYNCPOINT
              ROLLBACK at line 4100 on the failed rewrite. Here that check is
              expressed as a JPA version column and the 409 comes from the
              shared handler.
        WHY : Trade-offs: ${project.version} couples this module to the
              reactor's version and makes it unbuildable on its own. That is
              accepted because carddemo-services is never published, so a
              literal version would buy nothing and add a second value to
              forget to update.
        NOTE: there is deliberately NO dependency on auth-service,
              card-service, transaction-service, reference-service,
              batch-service, authorization-service or reporting-service.
              Importing another service's domain package is forbidden and is to
              be enforced mechanically rather than by review, by the rule set
              authored at
              services/common-lib/src/test/java/com/carddemo/common/architecture/LayeringRulesTest.java
              and re-run per module against its own classes. That class is
              authored at a later index of the same plan and does not exist yet,
              so at this checkpoint the boundary is carried by review; the
              test-scoped ArchUnit declaration further down is what lets this
              module run its own copy when it lands.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
            <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        =============================================================================
         Spring Boot starters: declared explicitly, versions inherited
        =============================================================================
        WHY : Assumptions: NOTHING in this block arrives transitively.
              services/common-lib/pom.xml marks its own copies of
              spring-boot-starter-web, spring-boot-starter-validation,
              spring-boot-starter-security and
              spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server as optional, and
              Maven does not propagate optional dependencies to consumers. A
              reader who assumes transitivity and deletes a "redundant"
              starter below breaks the compile of every controller and config
              class in this module, so each one is restated here on purpose.
        WHY : Assumptions: every version comes from
              spring-boot-starter-parent 4.1.0 by way of the aggregator.
              Adding a version element here would pin this module
              independently of the other eight and reintroduce exactly the
              drift a single parent exists to prevent.
        -->

        <!--
        WHY : Assumptions: beyond the MVC stack, this starter is what puts a
              Jackson ObjectMapper in the context for common-lib's MoneyModule
              to register into. That registration is what makes every monetary
              field serialise as a JSON string rather than a JSON number, so
              removing this starter would not merely disable the controllers,
              it would silently remove the money encoding guarantee.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        WHY : Assumptions: supplies the JPA optimistic locking this module
              depends on. The version column on accounts and customers is the
              native expression of the before image comparison COACTUPC
              performs by hand, so the concurrency behaviour is inherited from
              this starter rather than coded here.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        WHY : Assumptions: carries the Jakarta Validation constraints that the
              sixteen paragraph validation family of app/cbl/COACTUPC.cbl is
              transcribed into, running from 1200-EDIT-MAP-INPUTS through
              1280-EDIT-US-STATE-ZIP-CD. Those constraints are the parity
              surface for account update, so this starter is load bearing
              rather than convenience.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        WHY : Assumptions: the filter chain this starter provides is what
              replaces the client echoed CDEMO-USER-TYPE field of the CICS
              communication area. In the baseline that field was storage the
              terminal handed back, so a client could in principle assert its
              own privilege level; here authority comes from a signed token
              the client cannot forge.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        WHY : Assumptions: validates the Cognito issued JWT. SecurityConfig
              pairs it with common-lib's JwtRoleConverter, which turns the
              cognito:groups claim into Spring Security authorities and so
              preserves the administrator and user split the baseline encoded
              as SEC-USR-TYPE values A and U.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        WHY : Assumptions: the actuator health endpoint is probed by BOTH the
              ALB target group and the HEALTHCHECK instruction in this
              module's Dockerfile, so container level and load balancer level
              health can never disagree about the same instance. Dropping this
              starter breaks both probes at once, and the container would
              report itself unhealthy rather than fail loudly at startup.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        =============================================================================
         AWS integration: SQS plus wholly externalised configuration
        =============================================================================
        WHY : Alternatives Considered: the legacy
              org.springframework.cloud:spring-cloud-aws-* coordinate was
              evaluated and rejected because it is unmaintained and publishes
              nothing compatible with Spring Boot 4.1.0. io.awspring.cloud is
              the maintained line, and because the parent imports its bill of
              materials no version appears on any of the three entries below.
        WHY : Assumptions: Parameter Store and Secrets Manager are the ONLY
              sources of the datasource URL, the queue identifiers, the
              Cognito issuer URI and the encryption key identifiers. Not one
              of those values may be hardcoded, here or in any application
              profile. The baseline already proves that endpoints are
              configuration rather than code:
              app/app-vsam-mq/cbl/COACCT01.cbl physical line 92 declares
              01 QUEUE-INFO. holding four PIC X(48) queue name fields at lines
              93 to 96, every one initialised VALUE SPACES and filled at run
              time.
        WHY : Assumptions: NO transactional outbox artifact is required here,
              which is the exact opposite of authorization-service. COACCT01
              sets MQGMO-SYNCPOINT at physical line 347 before the get and
              MQPMO-SYNCPOINT at physical line 475 before the put, so get,
              business logic and put already form one atomic unit of work and
              there is no lost reply window to close.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-sqs</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-parameter-store</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-secrets-manager</artifactId>
        </dependency>


        <!--
        =============================================================================
         Persistence driver and schema migration
        =============================================================================
        WHY : Assumptions: the driver is runtime scope because no application
              class names a PostgreSQL type. Keeping it off the compile
              classpath means a stray import of a vendor specific class cannot
              creep into the domain or service layer, which is the same
              boundary the ArchUnit rules assert.
        WHY : Assumptions: flyway-database-postgresql is MANDATORY, not
              optional. Flyway 10 and later moved PostgreSQL support out of
              flyway-core, so core on its own fails at RUN time and not at
              compile time. Omitting the companion therefore produces a green
              build and an image that dies on first startup when it tries to
              migrate, which is the most expensive possible place to discover
              it. Both are left at the default compile scope on purpose so
              that a mis scoped runtime entry cannot quietly drop out of the
              packaged jar.
        WHY : Assumptions: this module owns exactly one migration,
              V1__account.sql, creating accounts, customers and card_xref
              inside the account schema. The schema itself, its owning role
              and its grants are bootstrapped separately by
              data-migration/sql/V0__schemas_and_roles.sql, so the migration
              here must never attempt to create a schema; the two would then
              race during a parallel deployment.
        WHY : Refactoring Rationale: encryption at rest and point in time
              recovery are corrections rather than ports. Every CICS file
              resource behind these tables is defined with no recovery and no
              journalling in app/csd/CARDDEMO.CSD, at line 9 for ACCTDAT,
              line 46 for CCXREF, line 59 for CUSTDAT and line 72 for CXACAIX,
              so there was nothing to carry across. Aurora supplies both, and
              the customer identifier columns land as encrypted BYTEA.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
            <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>
        <!--
        WHY : Assumptions: the STARTER is what activates migration, and naming it
              rather than flyway-core is the load bearing half of this pair.
              Spring Boot 4 split its autoconfiguration into one module per
              technology, and FlywayAutoConfiguration now ships in
              spring-boot-flyway, which only this starter puts on the classpath.
              flyway-core alone resolves, compiles and starts, and then applies
              no migration at all: the spring.flyway.* keys bind to nothing, the
              schema history table is never created, and the first query fails on
              a missing relation. Nothing in the build reports it.
        WHY : Refactoring Rationale: flyway-core is no longer declared directly.
              The starter brings it transitively at the version the aggregator's
              flyway.version property pins, so a direct declaration would add a
              second editable version site while contributing nothing to the
              classpath.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-flyway</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
            <artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        =============================================================================
         Published API contract and metric export
        =============================================================================
        WHY : Assumptions: springdoc serves the OpenAPI 3.1 contract kept at
              src/main/resources/openapi/account-api.yaml. That contract is a
              cross boundary commitment rather than documentation, because its
              consumer is a separately built and separately deployed artifact:
              services/authorization-service/src/main/java/com/carddemo/
              authorization/service/RestAccountContextClient.java, which builds
              each path itself and deserialises each response into its own
              record. Dropping this dependency would leave that client calling
              a contract nothing publishes.
        WHY : Refactoring Rationale: the consumer named above WAS ui/src/api/
              accounts.ts, a browser client, and no such file exists or will.
              This contract titles itself an internal read API and its three
              operations are governed by
              src/main/java/com/carddemo/account/config/InternalApiSecurityConfig.java,
              an ordered filter chain requiring a machine token minted by the
              calling service, which refuses the identity provider token every
              browser holds. ui/src/api/contracts.test.ts asserts that no
              browser client addresses any path this contract declares, so the
              exclusion is enforced rather than merely intended. Naming a
              browser as the consumer would have sent a reader looking for a
              module that is deliberately absent.
        WHY : Assumptions: the registry is runtime scope because no application
              class references a Prometheus type directly. Common tags are
              applied centrally by common-lib's MetricsConfig, so the only
              thing this module has to contribute is the exporter on the
              classpath.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
            <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
            <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        =============================================================================
         Test scope: unit, security, integration and architecture
        =============================================================================
        WHY : Assumptions: the parent already splits the two test phases, so
              nothing below needs a plugin binding here. Surefire runs *Test in
              the test phase and Failsafe runs *RepositoryIT in integration
              test and verify, writing to target/surefire-reports and
              target/failsafe-reports. Those two directories are collected by
              .github/workflows/services-ci.yml, itself authored at a later index
              of the same plan, and must never be relocated.
        -->

        <!--
        WHY : Assumptions: supplies JUnit 5, Mockito, AssertJ and MockMvc, the
              last of which is what lets the controller tests be sliced with
              @WebMvcTest instead of booting the full context and a database
              for what are request and response assertions.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        WHY : Assumptions: the controller tests must assert the administrator
              and user authority split that replaces the client echoed
              CDEMO-USER-TYPE field, and that needs the mock JWT and mock
              authority support this artifact provides. Without it the only way
              to exercise an authority boundary would be to obtain a real token
              from an identity provider during the test run, which would make
              the suite depend on a live external service.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-security-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        WHY : Alternatives Considered: an in memory database, H2 in particular,
              was evaluated and rejected because the *RepositoryIT classes are to
              exercise real PostgreSQL behaviour that H2 does not reproduce:
              NUMERIC(12,2) fixed point semantics, BYTEA columns, a genuine
              non unique secondary index standing in for the CXACAIX alternate
              index, and JPA version conflict detection under a real
              concurrent update. A test that passes on H2 and fails on Aurora
              is worse than no test, because it reads as parity evidence.
        WHY : Assumptions: the artifactIds carry a testcontainers prefix
              because Testcontainers 2.x renamed every module. The bare 1.x
              names junit-jupiter, postgresql and localstack are absent from
              testcontainers-bom 2.0.5, so declaring those spellings without a
              version yields a fatal missing version model error and the build
              never starts. Verified against the managed artifactId list of
              org/testcontainers/testcontainers-bom/2.0.5 and independently
              against spring-boot-dependencies 4.1.0, which sets the managed
              Testcontainers version to 2.0.5.
        WHY : Assumptions: the LocalStack module is here because the inquiry
              listener test needs a real SQS endpoint to observe delete on
              success and visibility timeout behaviour. A mocked client would
              assert that the code called a method, not that a message was
              actually removed from a queue, and redelivery is precisely the
              behaviour being preserved from the baseline.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
            <artifactId>testcontainers-junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
            <artifactId>testcontainers-postgresql</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
            <artifactId>testcontainers-localstack</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        WHY : Assumptions: ArchUnit turns three architectural boundaries into
              build failures rather than review comments: no AWS SDK type and
              no web type inside a domain package, no import of another
              service's domain package, and no float or double anywhere in the
              money path. The last is the one that matters most here, because
              a floating point balance produces plausible numbers that are
              quietly wrong, and ACCT-CURR-BAL PIC S9(10)V99 at
              app/cpy/CVACT01Y.cpy line 7 is exact fixed point by contract.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
            <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
        WHY : Refactoring Rationale: the engine above was declared on its own,
              on the understanding that the rule class authored in common-lib
              applied here by inheritance. It did not. Maven resolution passes a
              module's main classes to its consumers and never its test classes,
              so the class was evaluated against common-lib alone and the engine
              here had no rules to run; a float on a balance field in this
              module's domain package would have entered a green build. This
              artifact is how the class travels, and the architecture-rules
              Surefire execution in services/pom.xml is what runs it here,
              against this module's own compiled classes.
              Assumptions: ownership does not move. The rules stay authored once,
              at
              services/common-lib/src/test/java/com/carddemo/common/architecture/LayeringRulesTest.java,
              and this declaration adds no second copy. Version and scope are
              managed by the aggregator, so neither appears here and neither can
              drift module by module.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
            <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
            <type>test-jar</type>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

    </dependencies>

    <!--
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
     DELIBERATE EXCLUSIONS
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
     Everything named in this block is ABSENT ON PURPOSE. Each entry records
     why, so that a later reader does not add it back as an oversight. Nothing
     here appears in any dependency or plugin element above or below.

     Lombok
       Alternatives Considered: Lombok was evaluated and rejected because
       generated accessors cannot carry the Javadoc that Rule 1's validation
       gate requires, and the inherited Checkstyle configuration runs
       MissingJavadocMethod without allowing missing property Javadoc, so every
       generated getter would fail the gate. Java 21 record types plus explicit
       constructors give the same brevity with members that can be documented.

     MapStruct
       Alternatives Considered: rejected for two independent reasons. First, its
       most recent published release is a beta. Second and decisively, the
       copybook to DTO mapping in this module is not mechanical: it drops
       FILLER (for example FILLER PIC X(178) at app/cpy/CVACT01Y.cpy line 17
       and FILLER PIC X(168) at app/cpy/CVCUS01Y.cpy line 23), masks the
       primary account number to its last four digits, never serialises a card
       verification value, encrypts CUST-SSN and CUST-GOVT-ISSUED-ID, and
       renames the misspelled ACCT-EXPIRAION-DATE of app/cpy/CVACT01Y.cpy
       line 11. Every one of those decisions needs an inline justification at
       the mapping site, and a generated mapper has nowhere to hold it.

     resilience4j, spring-retry, and any circuit breaker
       Alternatives Considered: io.github.resilience4j:resilience4j-spring-boot3
       targets Spring Boot 3.x and was rejected on that basis, and the
       superseded spring-retry was rejected because Spring Framework 7, which
       arrives through the Boot 4.1.0 parent, moved retry into the framework
       core. Any class here that needs declarative retry uses @Retryable with
       the attribute maxRetries, where total attempts equal one plus that
       value, enabled by @EnableResilientMethods on a configuration class. It
       is NOT @EnableRetry and NOT maxAttempts; those are the older API and
       will not activate. A circuit breaker is omitted as well, because the only
       synchronous hops are inside the VPC behind an internal load balancer
       with bounded timeouts, so a breaker would add a failure mode without
       removing one.

     spring-boot-starter-batch
       Assumptions: chunk oriented jobs and the durable job repository belong
       to batch-service. This module has no BatchConfig and no job repository,
       and the posting unit of work that needs cross schema write grants is
       owned there, not here.

     redis, ElastiCache, kafka and kinesis clients
       Assumptions: the baseline has neither a cache tier nor a streaming
       platform, so neither is needed for parity. The messaging requirement is
       request and reply, which SQS satisfies through the starter declared
       above.

     A read replica or any second datasource
       Trade-offs: a replica would add cost and replica lag semantics for no
       parity benefit, since the baseline has no such split. Reporting reads
       are served from the writer through read only cross schema views owned by
       reporting-service, which keeps every read consistent with the write that
       preceded it.
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    -->

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!--
            WHY : Assumptions: the repackage goal rewrites the plain library
                  jar into the executable jar whose manifest names the boot
                  launcher, and that jar is the single artifact the runtime
                  stage of this module's Dockerfile copies. Without this plugin
                  the module still builds, so the omission would surface only
                  as a container that starts and immediately reports no main
                  manifest attribute.
            WHY : Assumptions: maven-checkstyle-plugin, maven-surefire-plugin
                  and maven-failsafe-plugin are configured once in the parent
                  at services/pom.xml and inherited here UNCHANGED. In
                  particular the Checkstyle execution named
                  checkstyle-documentation-gate is bound to the validate phase
                  so the documentation rule is enforced on every local build
                  and not only in CI, it runs the Checkstyle engine against
                  config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml, and it stops the build on
                  any violation at warning severity or above. Re declaring any
                  of those three here would either duplicate the binding, so
                  the gate runs twice, or silently loosen it, so please do not.
                  For the same reason no suppressions location is set here: the
                  suppression set is reached only through the suppression
                  filter inside checkstyle.xml, which resolves it from the
                  configuration directory and is declared non optional so a
                  missing file fails the build instead of disabling
                  suppression.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
